Do not trust errno after isatty() on Cygwin.

Cygwin's isatty() returns 0 for a descriptor that is not a terminal (a pipe
or file) without setting ENOTTY: errno is left unset or holds the result of an
unrelated internal lookup (ENOENT), so fdterm() threw std::ios_base::failure
at the start of b-boot. Treat everything but EBADF as "not a terminal".

--- a/libbutl/libbutl/fdstream.cxx	2026-11-18 13:43:09.043471080 +0900
+++ b/libbutl/libbutl/fdstream.cxx	2026-11-18 13:43:09.222732020 +0900
@@ -1474,11 +1474,25 @@
   bool
   fdterm (int fd)
   {
+#ifdef __CYGWIN__
+    errno = 0;
+#endif
+
     int r (isatty (fd));
 
     if (r == 1)
       return true;
 
+#ifdef __CYGWIN__
+    // Cygwin's isatty() doesn't report a descriptor that is not a terminal
+    // (a pipe, for example) with ENOTTY: errno is left unset or holds the
+    // result of an unrelated internal lookup (ENOENT). Only EBADF is a real
+    // error.
+    //
+    if (errno != EBADF)
+      return false;
+#endif
+
     // POSIX specifies ENOTTY errno code for an indication that the descriptor
     // doesn't refer to a terminal. However, both Linux and FreeBSD man pages
     // also mention EINVAL for this case.
